{:db/id 17592186124593 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id 17592186124404 :question/id #uuid "c623bb85-401d-5d00-b4a8-cb86570d3304" :question/math-academy-id "e-8282" :question/problem "Evaluate $\\dfrac {\\textrm d}{\\textrm d t}[f(\\mathbf r(t))]$ at $t = \\pi$ given that\n$\\nabla f(x,y) = (2y - 1) \\, \\mathbf i + (2x-1) \\, \\mathbf j$\nand\n$\\mathbf r (t) = \\sin{t} \\, \\mathbf i + \\cos{t} \\, \\mathbf j.$" :question/worked-solution "The chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\textrm d}{\\textrm d t} [f(\\mathbf r (t))] = \\nabla f(\\mathbf r(t)) \\cdot \\mathbf r'(t).\n$$\n\nIn addition, we've been given that\n\n$$\n\\nabla f(x,y) = (2y - 1) \\, \\mathbf i + (2x - 1) \\, \\mathbf j.\n$$\n\nFirst, we substitute $x = \\sin{t}$ and $y = \\cos{t}$ into $\\nabla f(x,y)$ to find $\\nabla f(\\mathbf{r}(t)){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t)) & =(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-1)\\,i+(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-1)\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $\\mathbf{r}(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,j \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-1\\,i+(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-1)\\,j)\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,j) \\\\  & =(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-1)\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡t+(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-1)\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\  & =2{cos}^{2}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-2{sin}^{2}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\  & =2({cos}^{2}⁡t-{sin}^{2}⁡t)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the above at $t = \\pi,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(\\pi ))] & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\pi +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi -\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi  \\\\  & =2(1)+0-(-1) \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cc017b0d-6418-570a-9a5d-57572e4fe70a" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id 17592186124956 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "05130439-f8ad-50ab-88fd-4aef2c512276" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id 17592186321194} {:answer/id #uuid "2ab62fb4-96f6-544e-a43f-7c54848d7b96" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id 17592186321475} {:answer/id #uuid "2bb77509-e2b1-57fe-aa81-c820e1759f9a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id 17592186321484} {:answer/id #uuid "dd29a8cb-f3e2-55a6-b61e-7dcb1a986936" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id 17592186322877} {:answer/id #uuid "eb8e8763-9fc2-54d7-beb9-a7f164362324" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id 17592186322991}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dd29a8cb-f3e2-55a6-b61e-7dcb1a986936" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id 17592186322877} :answer-field/id #uuid "b0f23e9e-f039-5d42-b46f-a3ba271cfc87" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186323513}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ceb16fee-64a8-51ee-a3b7-7009e970259a" :question/math-academy-id "q-36215" :question/problem "Evaluate $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]$ at $t=1$ given that\n$∇f(x,y)=\\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+3y}\\,i+\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}+3y}\\,j$\nand\n$r(t)=t\\,i+{t}^{2}\\,j.$" :question/worked-solution "The chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]=∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nIn addition, we've been given that\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=\\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+3y}\\,i+\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}+3y}\\,j.\n$$\n\nFirst, we substitute $x=t$ and $y={t}^{2}$ into $∇f(x,y)$ to find $∇f(r(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t)) & =\\frac{2(t)}{(t)^{2}+3({t}^{2})}\\,i+\\frac{3}{(t)^{2}+3({t}^{2})}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{2t}{{t}^{2}+3{t}^{2}}\\,i+\\frac{3}{{t}^{2}+3{t}^{2}}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{2t}{4{t}^{2}}\\,i+\\frac{3}{4{t}^{2}}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2t}\\,i+\\frac{3}{4{t}^{2}}\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $r(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2})\\,j \\\\  & =i+2t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{2t}\\,i+\\frac{3}{4{t}^{2}}\\,j)\\cdot (i+2t\\,j) \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{2t})\\cdot 1+(\\frac{3}{4{t}^{2}})\\cdot 2t \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2t}+\\frac{3}{2t} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{2t} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the above at $t=1,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(1))] & =\\frac{2}{1}=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id 17592186125246 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "15d810bf-905d-5d78-ba48-12ddc9b892e0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2-\\pi " :db/id 17592186357945} {:answer/id #uuid "2a6ad6c1-0c8d-5fb0-b435-dee857f73211" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id 17592186358135} {:answer/id #uuid "7ae9204f-c34d-510e-94bc-023fcf5f25fd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{4}" :db/id 17592186358819} {:answer/id #uuid "9af214c2-c6da-5e82-8df8-28a9a5b98d27" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id 17592186359108} {:answer/id #uuid "acbb10f0-bfb6-5636-9744-b6306c87e6ac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id 17592186359243}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9af214c2-c6da-5e82-8df8-28a9a5b98d27" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id 17592186359108} :answer-field/id #uuid "b1da5512-5fc1-5bb7-8ab0-03b3b2b0ba70" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186360309}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "72c15c65-cd14-5a6c-9b4a-a63cd2f705de" :question/math-academy-id "q-94872" :question/problem "Evaluate $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]$ at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ given that $∇f(x,y)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,i+x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\,j$ and $r(t)=\\frac{t}{2}\\,i+t\\,j.$" :question/worked-solution "The chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]=∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nIn addition, we've been given that\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,i+x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\,j.\n$$\n\nFirst, we substitute $x=\\frac{t}{2}$ and $y=t$ into $∇f(x,y)$ to find $∇f(r(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t)) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+\\frac{t}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $r(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{t}{2})\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\,i+j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+\\frac{t}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j)\\cdot (\\frac{1}{2}\\,i+j) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{t}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the above at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(\\frac{\\pi }{2}))] & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})+\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(1+\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\cdot 0) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id 17592186284585 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "439aee45-933e-5525-9345-5b17029a63c9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\pi " :db/id 17592186282266} {:answer/id #uuid "582ea5c1-1e57-5176-9167-972093895fd3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id 17592186282440} {:answer/id #uuid "7eb3e4fe-ec5b-5339-9652-4a5cb0a96a8b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi " :db/id 17592186282782} {:answer/id #uuid "848c5f97-904d-5825-a1c2-c3a1c330760f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id 17592186282831} {:answer/id #uuid "e9802ab6-19b7-5148-b0e2-623781519ded" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id 17592186283729}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "848c5f97-904d-5825-a1c2-c3a1c330760f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id 17592186282831} :answer-field/id #uuid "353e1da0-d350-56e5-9994-3f64a6bca475" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186284029}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d2df41f1-db14-5e76-bbfb-6cc0c8cd31a4" :question/math-academy-id "q-167497" :question/problem "Evaluate $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]$ at $t=\\pi $ given that $∇f(x,y)=2xy\\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}^{2})\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{2})\\,j$ and $r(t)=\\sqrt{t}\\,i+\\frac{t}{2}\\,j.$" :question/worked-solution "The chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]=∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nIn addition, we've been given that\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=2xy\\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}^{2})\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{2})\\,j.\n$$\n\nFirst, we substitute $x=\\sqrt{t}$ and $y=\\frac{t}{2}$ into $∇f(x,y)$ to find $∇f(r(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t)) & ={t}^{3/2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $r(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\sqrt{t})\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{t}{2})\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{t}}\\,i+\\frac{1}{2}j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =({t}^{3/2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)\\,j)\\cdot (\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{t}}\\,i+\\frac{1}{2}j) \\\\  & ={t}^{3/2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)\\cdot \\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{t}}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the above at $t=\\pi ,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(\\pi ))] & =\\frac{\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi }{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi (-1)+0}{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id 17592186284586 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "29453b2c-6d17-5788-a24a-a3018b5c97bb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id 17592186282046} {:answer/id #uuid "2c1dd875-1ab5-5d5c-8cee-d4a760e0fad1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{e}" :db/id 17592186282077} {:answer/id #uuid "36e21432-61e2-5f75-bf7b-6c17d3bac35a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2e" :db/id 17592186282176} {:answer/id #uuid "878e3c0b-0fa7-530a-8b50-3b0123c90209" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e" :db/id 17592186282857} {:answer/id #uuid "f3ea2448-8707-56af-883d-3fdfe6fbad5d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id 17592186283819}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "29453b2c-6d17-5788-a24a-a3018b5c97bb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id 17592186282046} :answer-field/id #uuid "99251eef-a601-5418-8667-74393704a93e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186284194}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "15c14f08-def7-5eb6-8ced-3b42bca65403" :question/math-academy-id "q-167498" :question/problem "Evaluate $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]$ at $t=-1$ given that $∇f(x,y)=2x{e}^{{x}^{2}+y}\\,i+{e}^{{x}^{2}+y}\\,j$ and $r(t)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\text{ }i+(t+{cos}^{2}⁡t)\\text{ }j.$" :question/worked-solution "The chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]=∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nIn addition, we've been given that\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=2x{e}^{{x}^{2}+y}\\,i+{e}^{{x}^{2}+y}\\,j.\n$$\n\nFirst, we substitute $x=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t$ and $y=t+{cos}^{2}⁡t$ into $∇f(x,y)$ to find $∇f(r(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t)) & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,{e}^{{sin}^{2}⁡t+t+{cos}^{2}⁡t}\\,i+{e}^{{sin}^{2}⁡t+t+{cos}^{2}⁡t}\\,j \\\\  & =2{e}^{t+1}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+{e}^{t+1}\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $r(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t+{cos}^{2}⁡t)\\,j \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+(1-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =(2{e}^{t+1}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+{e}^{t+1}\\,j)\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+(1-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,j) \\\\  & =2{e}^{t+1}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡t+{e}^{t+1}\\cdot (1-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t) \\\\  & =2{e}^{t+1}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+{e}^{t+1}-2{e}^{t+1}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\  & ={e}^{t+1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the above at $t=-1,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(-1))] & ={e}^{-1+1}=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id 17592186323880 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0b203b6e-7830-50da-aa18-f454f862a948" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id 17592186321238} {:answer/id #uuid "2cf6d8fc-e1b8-5fdc-b9b5-09f8f6941c20" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi +2}{\\pi }" :db/id 17592186321497} {:answer/id #uuid "c175b73f-e2fb-54e8-a7e0-b2906ff65bfd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi +1}{\\pi }" :db/id 17592186322651} {:answer/id #uuid "c8d08b91-916d-5adb-a62d-964ea2cc4e5d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{\\pi +2}" :db/id 17592186322710} {:answer/id #uuid "e1542b36-0e72-5814-8c1f-4765946bc3e9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{\\pi +1}" :db/id 17592186322908}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2cf6d8fc-e1b8-5fdc-b9b5-09f8f6941c20" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi +2}{\\pi }" :db/id 17592186321497} :answer-field/id #uuid "276fef95-8127-5751-a8f3-63d27774306c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186323248}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "fdba027b-12bc-5b22-aa20-f040a6af0b36" :question/math-academy-id "q-37106" :question/problem "Evaluate $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]$ at $t=1$ given that $∇f(x,y)=\\frac{1}{x}\\,i+\\frac{1}{y}\\,j$ and $r(t)=(2{t}^{2}+2)\\,i+\\operatorname{arctan}⁡t\\,j.$" :question/worked-solution "The chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]=∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nIn addition, we've been given that\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=\\frac{1}{x}\\,i+\\frac{1}{y}\\,j.\n$$\n\nFirst, we substitute $x=2{t}^{2}+2$ and $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡t$ into $∇f(x,y)$ to find $∇f(r(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\,f(r(t)) & =\\frac{1}{2{t}^{2}+2}\\text{ }i+\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{arctan}⁡t}\\text{ }j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $r(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2{t}^{2}+2)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡t)\\,j \\\\  & =4t\\,i+\\frac{1}{1+{t}^{2}}\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{2{t}^{2}+2}\\,i+\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{arctan}⁡t}\\,j)\\cdot (4t\\,i+\\frac{1}{1+{t}^{2}}\\,j) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2{t}^{2}+2}\\cdot 4t+\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{arctan}⁡t}\\cdot \\frac{1}{1+{t}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2t}{{t}^{2}+1}+\\frac{1}{({t}^{2}+1)\\operatorname{arctan}⁡t} \\\\  & =\\frac{2t\\operatorname{arctan}⁡t+1}{({t}^{2}+1)\\operatorname{arctan}⁡t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the above at $t=1,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(1))] & =\\frac{2(1)\\operatorname{arctan}⁡1+1}{({1}^{2}+1)\\operatorname{arctan}⁡1} \\\\  & =\\frac{(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4}+1)}{(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4})} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi +2}{\\pi }.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id 17592186323883 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2c36ce21-00fb-5fff-a854-09a22fe9b12a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id 17592186321487} {:answer/id #uuid "3e8cc371-8d3d-50ee-84ac-7aa30e752029" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id 17592186321639} {:answer/id #uuid "51cf7225-599b-5037-bcde-98e28ee76a46" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id 17592186321791} {:answer/id #uuid "842b9eff-5030-5ffd-9f05-2ac210a4ff66" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value